1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,360
Australia banned asbestos at the end of 2003.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,880
Yet each year, hundreds of Australians are still

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,560
diagnosed with mesothelioma, a cancer that asbestos can

4
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:11,680
cause.

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,160
So how can a harm show up decades

6
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:16,000
after the exposure stopped?

7
00:00:16,121 --> 00:00:18,000
And why do some people who breathed the

8
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:20,480
same dust never get sick?

9
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:24,400
That's today's question: how does environmental exposure cause

10
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:25,280
disease?

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,100
Earlier, you sorted the causes of non-infectious disease,

12
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:33,540
and environmental causes come from outside the body.

13
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:36,180
Here's how they actually make people sick.

14
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:38,740
Every case follows one spine.

15
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:42,260
First, an exposure: real contact with a harmful

16
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:45,340
agent, including how much and for how long.

17
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:48,580
Next, a change in cells or tissue.

18
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:50,420
Then, an effect on health.

19
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,220
Let's run tobacco smoke through it, because one

20
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:56,100
exposure can lead to three different diseases.

21
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,700
Pathway one is lung cancer.

22
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:03,620
Smoke carries carcinogens, chemicals that raise the risk

23
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:04,340
of cancer.

24
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:07,140
They reach the cells lining the airways and

25
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:08,660
attach to their DNA.

26
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:11,620
You've seen what happens next: if that damage

27
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,340
is copied before it's repaired, it becomes a

28
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:15,140
mutation.

29
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:18,180
When mutations build up in genes that control

30
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:20,900
cell division, a cell can start dividing when

31
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:21,620
it shouldn't.

32
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:23,860
That can be the start of a tumour.

33
00:01:25,067 --> 00:01:27,986
Pathway two doesn't need a mutation at all.

34
00:01:28,267 --> 00:01:31,466
Smoke irritates the airways, so they stay inflamed

35
00:01:31,547 --> 00:01:32,346
for years.

36
00:01:32,987 --> 00:01:35,706
White blood cells gather and release enzymes that

37
00:01:35,787 --> 00:01:38,266
digest the thin walls between the tiny air

38
00:01:38,347 --> 00:01:38,906
sacs.

39
00:01:39,547 --> 00:01:42,826
Many small sacs merge into fewer, bigger ones,

40
00:01:42,907 --> 00:01:45,386
so there's much less surface for gas exchange.

41
00:01:46,027 --> 00:01:48,906
Mucus builds up too, because the hair-like cilia

42
00:01:48,987 --> 00:01:50,746
that sweep it out are damaged.

43
00:01:51,387 --> 00:01:56,666
Together this is COPD, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease,

44
00:01:56,747 --> 00:01:58,906
and the lost walls don't grow back.

45
00:02:00,134 --> 00:02:02,813
Pathway three is in the blood vessels.

46
00:02:03,094 --> 00:02:05,733
Chemicals in smoke damage the smooth lining of

47
00:02:05,814 --> 00:02:06,613
the arteries.

48
00:02:07,094 --> 00:02:09,653
Fatty deposits and white blood cells build up

49
00:02:09,734 --> 00:02:12,373
at those damaged spots, forming a plaque that

50
00:02:12,454 --> 00:02:13,573
narrows the artery.

51
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:17,653
Meanwhile, carbon monoxide from the smoke takes oxygen's

52
00:02:17,814 --> 00:02:20,613
place on haemoglobin, so the blood carries less

53
00:02:20,694 --> 00:02:24,253
oxygen, and nicotine makes the heart beat faster.

54
00:02:24,534 --> 00:02:27,493
A narrowed artery and a harder-working heart raise

55
00:02:27,574 --> 00:02:29,573
the risk of a heart attack or stroke.

56
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:33,440
So what decides how much risk?

57
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:36,880
First, dose: how much of the agent gets

58
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:38,400
in, over how long.

59
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:42,240
For smoking, it's counted in pack-years, packs per

60
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,760
day multiplied by years smoked.

61
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:47,120
One pack a day for twenty years is

62
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:48,320
twenty pack-years.

63
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,440
For cancer, think of each exposure as a

64
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,360
ticket in a lottery you don't want to

65
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:53,840
win.

66
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:57,120
Most tickets lose, because the damage is repaired.

67
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,080
But more tickets mean more chances that one

68
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:02,720
cell collects several unlucky changes.

69
00:03:03,934 --> 00:03:05,373
Second, time.

70
00:03:05,454 --> 00:03:08,653
Those changes take years to build up, so

71
00:03:08,734 --> 00:03:11,373
there's usually a long gap between exposure and

72
00:03:11,454 --> 00:03:14,093
diagnosis, called latency.

73
00:03:14,414 --> 00:03:16,413
Asbestos shows it clearly.

74
00:03:16,494 --> 00:03:18,973
Its fibres are long and thin, and once

75
00:03:19,054 --> 00:03:21,213
breathed in, they can lodge in the lining

76
00:03:21,334 --> 00:03:22,653
around the lungs.

77
00:03:22,774 --> 00:03:26,013
Immune cells called macrophages try to swallow them,

78
00:03:26,094 --> 00:03:29,533
can't, and keep releasing reactive chemicals that damage

79
00:03:29,614 --> 00:03:32,173
nearby cells and their DNA.

80
00:03:32,254 --> 00:03:34,893
That slow damage can take twenty to fifty

81
00:03:34,974 --> 00:03:37,933
years to become mesothelioma, which is why the

82
00:03:38,014 --> 00:03:41,053
2003 ban hasn't stopped new cases yet.

83
00:03:42,267 --> 00:03:44,666
So why do many heavy smokers never get

84
00:03:44,747 --> 00:03:47,466
lung cancer, while some people who've never smoked

85
00:03:47,627 --> 00:03:47,946
do?

86
00:03:48,427 --> 00:03:52,506
Because exposure raises the probability, not the certainty.

87
00:03:52,747 --> 00:03:55,306
DNA repair works better in some people than

88
00:03:55,547 --> 00:03:55,986
others.

89
00:03:56,187 --> 00:03:59,226
The immune system destroys some damaged cells.

90
00:03:59,307 --> 00:04:02,026
And which cell collects which mutation is partly

91
00:04:02,107 --> 00:04:02,866
chance.

92
00:04:03,147 --> 00:04:06,106
Never-smokers can be exposed in other ways too,

93
00:04:06,187 --> 00:04:09,866
through second-hand smoke, radon gas or polluted air.

94
00:04:09,907 --> 00:04:13,146
And exposures can add up: asbestos and smoking

95
00:04:13,227 --> 00:04:15,946
together raise lung cancer risk far more than

96
00:04:16,027 --> 00:04:16,906
either alone.

97
00:04:18,134 --> 00:04:20,693
That's also why a risk isn't a verdict

98
00:04:20,774 --> 00:04:21,893
on one person.

99
00:04:22,454 --> 00:04:25,973
Many exposures aren't chosen: a worker cutting asbestos

100
00:04:26,054 --> 00:04:28,933
sheeting, or a child breathing a parent's smoke.

101
00:04:29,414 --> 00:04:32,853
What reliably lowers disease is lowering exposure across

102
00:04:32,894 --> 00:04:34,213
a whole population.

103
00:04:34,774 --> 00:04:37,813
In 2025, about six in a hundred Australian

104
00:04:37,894 --> 00:04:40,693
adults smoked daily, and lung cancer rates in

105
00:04:40,774 --> 00:04:43,413
men have fallen too, decades behind.

106
00:04:43,974 --> 00:04:47,573
For UV, SunSmart's advice works the same way:

107
00:04:47,654 --> 00:04:50,773
slip, slop, slap, seek and slide.

108
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,440
Two exam questions.

109
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:54,480
First.

110
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:58,000
The graph shows illustrative trends for one population

111
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:00,680
of men: the percentage who smoked, and the

112
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:03,360
rate of new lung cancer cases, from 1945

113
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:05,840
to 2020.

114
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,000
Describe how the timing of the two trends

115
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:08,960
differs.

116
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,360
Then explain the difference.

117
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:12,720
Pause here to answer.

118
00:05:23,134 --> 00:05:27,213
Smoking fell steadily from 1945, but lung cancer

119
00:05:27,374 --> 00:05:31,213
kept rising until the early 1980s, then fell,

120
00:05:31,294 --> 00:05:32,653
decades behind.

121
00:05:32,974 --> 00:05:35,173
The explanation is latency.

122
00:05:35,454 --> 00:05:38,973
Mutations from earlier smoking were already building up,

123
00:05:39,054 --> 00:05:41,693
so cancers kept appearing long after people had

124
00:05:41,774 --> 00:05:44,333
quit, even while fewer young people took up

125
00:05:44,414 --> 00:05:44,893
smoking.

126
00:05:45,454 --> 00:05:48,333
The classic mistake is saying smoking can't cause

127
00:05:48,414 --> 00:05:50,733
lung cancer because the two peaks don't line

128
00:05:50,814 --> 00:05:51,213
up.

129
00:05:52,434 --> 00:05:52,993
Second.

130
00:05:53,474 --> 00:05:56,193
A gardener has worked outdoors in Queensland for

131
00:05:56,234 --> 00:05:58,833
thirty years without sun protection.

132
00:05:58,954 --> 00:06:02,113
Explain how this repeated UV exposure can increase

133
00:06:02,194 --> 00:06:03,873
the risk of melanoma.

134
00:06:03,954 --> 00:06:07,313
Then explain why a co-worker with similar exposure

135
00:06:07,434 --> 00:06:08,993
may never develop it.

136
00:06:09,154 --> 00:06:10,433
Pause here to answer.

137
00:06:20,834 --> 00:06:24,033
UV is absorbed by DNA in skin cells

138
00:06:24,114 --> 00:06:27,713
and joins neighbouring bases, so the DNA kinks.

139
00:06:28,034 --> 00:06:30,593
If that's copied before it's repaired, it becomes

140
00:06:30,634 --> 00:06:33,953
a mutation, and thirty years of exposure let

141
00:06:34,274 --> 00:06:37,153
mutations build up in genes that control division.

142
00:06:37,474 --> 00:06:39,633
But exposure raises the probability.

143
00:06:40,114 --> 00:06:43,953
Repair, immune clearance and chance differ between people.

144
00:06:44,594 --> 00:06:47,553
The classic mistake is writing that UV always

145
00:06:47,634 --> 00:06:48,753
causes cancer.

146
00:06:49,967 --> 00:06:53,326
So for any environmental disease question, name the

147
00:06:53,447 --> 00:06:56,366
exposure and its dose, describe the change in

148
00:06:56,447 --> 00:06:59,406
cells or tissue, link it to the effect,

149
00:06:59,487 --> 00:07:01,726
then explain why it's a risk, not a

150
00:07:01,807 --> 00:07:02,846
certainty.

151
00:07:03,327 --> 00:07:06,846
Next, an exposure nobody can avoid: what we

152
00:07:06,967 --> 00:07:09,886
eat, and how nutrition changes disease risk.

153
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:12,540
Here are the key notes.

154
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:14,700
Pause the video if you need more time

155
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:15,580
to copy them.
